{"abstract":[{"text":"Add NanoHTTP to a project, answer your first request and learn where to go next.","type":"text"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP"]]},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/GettingStarted"},"kind":"article","metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"NanoHTTP"}],"role":"article","roleHeading":"Article","title":"Getting Started"},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"anchor":"Overview","level":2,"text":"Overview","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"NanoHTTP is a Swift package without dependencies. It runs a web server inside your own process: a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"command-line tool, a Mac app, an iOS app. It supports macOS 12, iOS 15 and tvOS 15 and later.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Add-the-package","level":3,"text":"Add the package","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Add the package to the dependencies of your ","type":"text"},{"code":"Package.swift","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and the product ","type":"text"},{"code":"NanoHTTP","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" to your target:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":[".package(url: \"https://github.com/objecthub/swift-nanohttp.git\", from: \"2.1.0\"),","...",".product(name: \"NanoHTTP\", package: \"swift-nanohttp\"),"],"syntax":"text","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"In Xcode, choose File > Add Package Dependencies and enter the URL of the repository.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Your-first-server","level":3,"text":"Your first server","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A server maps methods and paths to ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"handlers","type":"text"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", functions that take a ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/NanoHTTPRequest","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"return a ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/NanoHTTPResponse","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":":","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["import Foundation","import NanoHTTP","","let server = NanoHTTPServer()","server.get[\"/hello\"] = { _ in .ok(.text(\"Hello, world!\")) }","server.get[\"/hello/:name\"] = { request in","  .ok(.text(\"Hello, \\(request.params[\"name\"] ?? \"stranger\")!\"))","}","try server.start(8080)","print(\"Serving at http://localhost:\\(try server.port())\")","RunLoop.main.run()"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/NanoHTTPServerIO/start(_:)","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" returns at once: the server accepts connections on threads of its own. A command-line tool has to keep the process alive, as the example does with","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the run loop; an app has a run loop anyway. Passing the port ","type":"text"},{"code":"0","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" lets the system choose a free port, which","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/NanoHTTPServerIO/port()","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" reports.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Try it from a terminal:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["curl http://localhost:8080/hello/Ada"],"syntax":"bash","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Stop the server with ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/NanoHTTPServerIO/stop()","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":". A stopped server can be started again.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Who-can-connect","level":3,"text":"Who can connect","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Without further settings the server accepts connections from ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"this machine only","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" (","type":"text"},{"code":"127.0.0.1","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"). A server that","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other devices are meant to reach has to say so:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"code":["let server = NanoHTTPServer()","server.configuration.listenAddress = .allInterfaces","try server.start(8080)"],"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"With ","type":"text"},{"code":".address(\"192.168.1.20\")","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" the server listens at one address; one that is not an address of this machine makes","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"start","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" throw instead of listening everywhere. See","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/SecurityAndLimits","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" for what else to consider before you expose a server.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"Next-steps","level":3,"text":"Next steps","type":"heading"},{"items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/Routing","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" explains how paths are matched and how variables work.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/HandlersAndResponses","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/ReadingRequests","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" cover what handlers receive and return.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/ServingFiles","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/WebSockets","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" cover files and live connections.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"https://objecthub.github.io/swift-nanohttp/documentation/nanohtml/","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", the second library of the package, builds","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"pages for your server; it has a documentation of its own.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/Examples","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" are six complete programs that you can run and change: a JSON API, a file server, a chat, and more.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/HowARequestIsServed","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" describes what happens between a client’s request and your handler.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"type":"unorderedList"}],"kind":"content"}],"references":{"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP":{"abstract":[{"text":"A small HTTP/1.1 server for apps and tools: routing, files, forms, uploads and WebSockets, without dependencies.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP","kind":"symbol","role":"collection","title":"NanoHTTP","type":"topic","url":"/documentation/nanohttp"},"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/Examples":{"abstract":[{"text":"Six small programs that show how the pieces fit together, and that you can run.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/Examples","kind":"article","role":"article","title":"Examples","type":"topic","url":"/documentation/nanohttp/examples"},"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/HandlersAndResponses":{"abstract":[{"text":"Answer requests with text, JSON, bytes, redirects or streamed content.","type":"text"}],"identifier":"doc://NanoHTTP/documentation/NanoHTTP/HandlersAndResponses","kind":"article","role":"collectionGroup","title":"Handlers and Responses","type":"topic","u